A||N||2||coachreed||rebuild on '86 SE||||1||
Z||000000||coachreed||09-24-2000||04:51 PM||||I am in the process of helping a friend with his '86 Fiero that is apparently locked up.  His folks bought it for cheap after the previous owner had the engine quit.  The car has sat for around 2 years and now we are taking care of things.  We tried turning the engine over with a breaker bar on the crank, but it wouldn't budge.  <P>I have a few questions to get this started.  First off, I know that the engine is suppoesed to come out the bottom, but I am a little miffed with this.  Once the cradle is lowered from the car, how the heck do you get the car high enough to slide the engine out?  Is it possible to pull the engine out the top?  It looks like it would be tight, but possible.  Anyone have suggestions on this?  We started removing parts this morning, but the rain put an end to our fun.<P>Any suggestions or info would be greatly appreciated.  Also, if we need a new short block or long block, where should be get this.  Discount Auto quoted around $1500 for a long block, but my friend would like to get it done cheaper.  Thanks in advance.<P>------------------<BR>Coach... I mean Randy (I often forget who I really am)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/coach_reed.geo" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/coach_reed.geo/images/coachbanner.jpg"> </A><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-24-2000||10:26 PM||||Here's how:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003751.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003751.html</A> <P>By the way, I've never done this to a Fiero  myself.  But when I do, I plan to bring "cradle and all" out the bottom. <P>FWIW, I <B>did</B>, however, pull the front end (engine+ trans.+ cradle+ struts) out of a Chevy Citation once.  I dropped the cradle, including the tires, down to the floor.<P>I had two guys to help.  The three of us grabbed the front bumper of the car, lifted the front end of the car up over the engine, and rolled the car out of the garage into the backyard like a rickshaw.  My girlfriend (and one of other guy's g/f) took up some old wheels, and laid them flat on the ground for us, so that we could set the car on them, like jackstands.  The whole thing took just a few minutes. <P>Obviously, you have to be very careful if you decide to try something like this, and make sure everyone understands that no one is to put any  part of their body under the vehicle at any time!<P>But it was kind of fun - sort of like a barn raising.  It helped that one of the other two guys was a bodybuilder type, and the other fellow was about twenty years old.  It wasn't  much effort at all for anyone, really - and truthfully, if it ever started to feel like we were straining ourselves, I would have called the whole thing off in a heartbeat.            <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||coachreed||09-25-2000||12:37 AM||||<B>UPDATE!!!</B><P>Anyone have a spare 2.5L 4 cylinder motor laying around?  One we pulled the exhaust manifold off, we found #3 rod peeking out the rear (or is the front... towards the front) of the motor.  Ouch!!!  Looks like the quest for a new power plant is in line.<P>Anyone who might have a motor laying around, please email me at  <A HREF="mailto:rreed5@home.com">rreed5@home.com</A>  and let me know.  If it matters, I am in Sarasota, Florida (Sunny SW Florida).  Anyone?<P>Randy... I mean Coach (I often forget who I really am)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
